<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Jolokia access policy for GeoNetwork.

  The agent exists here for one purpose: letting the admin dashboard and the
  editor board poll the progress of GeoNetwork's own long running tasks. It is
  reachable by any authenticated user, down to the RegisteredUser profile, so
  this file is what keeps the rest of the JVM out of reach.

  Three rules when editing it:

  1. Keep <commands> present and empty. Both halves matter, and deleting the
     element is a mistake: every command then becomes allowed "in
     general", the <mbeans> allow list stops being consulted, and the agent
     grants read, write and exec on every MBean in the JVM. Naming a command
     inside <commands> widens that one command past the allow list while the
     file still reads as though it were restricted; in particular, do not add
     <command>read</command>. An empty self-closing tag looks like cruft. It
     is the whole restriction.

  2. Keep the allow list scoped to the "geonetwork-*" domain, and keep its
     attributes read-only (mode="read"). The hyphen is load bearing:
     "geonetwork-*" does not match the plain "geonetwork" domain.

  3. Do not add a <deny> section, and do not rely on one being there: under
     rule 1 it is inert. Jolokia consults <deny> only for commands allowed in
     general, and none are, so a <deny> here changes nothing while reading as
     extra hardening. Deny by leaving things out of the allow list.

  What the allow list has to cover: the tasks register MBeans named
  "geonetwork-<catalogue-uuid>:name=<task>,idx=<n>", where <task> is currently
  one of "url-check" (MAnalyseProcess), "indexing-task"
  (BatchOpsMetadataReindexer) or "batch-etf-inspire"
  (MInspireEtfValidateProcess). The placeholder slots add two more shapes:
  EmptySlot registers "name=url-check,idx=empty-slot-<n>", and EmptySlotBatch
  drops the "name" key altogether, registering
  "geonetwork-<catalogue-uuid>:idx=empty-slot-<n>".

  Why the domain rather than the task: the task names above are fixed
  literals, so they could be listed one by one, but the catalogue uuid is only
  known at runtime, future tasks are not known at all, and one <mbean> entry
  per task would have to be kept in step with the code. So everything
  GeoNetwork registers under "geonetwork-<catalogue-uuid>" is readable,
  present and future: treat the whole domain as readable by any authenticated
  user, and keep anything worth protecting out of it.

  What the allow list bounds: attribute values, and only those. No attribute
  value outside it can be read, in any request form. It does not hide the
  existence of MBeans, and there is deliberately no <http> section confining
  the agent to GET, which is a trade-off rather than a no-op. Both points are
  spelled out, with the mechanism and the tighter alternative, in
  JolokiaAccessPolicyTest.patternReadEnumeratesMBeanNamesWithoutValues and
  httpMethodIsNotRestricted.

  JolokiaAccessPolicyTest covers all of the above. Run it after changing this
  file, and fix the policy rather than the test if it fails.
-->
<restrict>
    <!-- Deny every command. Must stay present and empty: deleting this
         element grants read, write and exec on every MBean in the JVM.
         See rule 1 above before editing. -->
    <commands/>

    <!-- Read-only access to GeoNetwork's own MBean domain, and nothing else. -->
    <mbeans>
        <mbean>
            <name>geonetwork-*:*</name>
            <attribute mode="read">*</attribute>
        </mbean>
    </mbeans>
</restrict>
